---
import ParadoxLayout from '../layouts/ParadoxLayout.astro';
---

<ParadoxLayout slug="simpsons-paradox">
    <p class="text-slate-600 mb-6">
        Simpson’s Paradox happens when a trend appears in several separate groups but reverses or
        disappears when the groups are combined. This usually happens because a hidden factor changes
        the relative sizes or makeup of the groups, making the overall average misleading.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 mb-6">
        Below are two groups of students, each showing a <strong>positive</strong> relationship
        between study hours and test scores: more studying means better scores within each group.
        But watch what happens when the two groups are combined into one: the overall trend
        <strong>reverses</strong>. None of the underlying data changes, only how it's grouped.
        Drag the slider slowly from left to right to watch the story unfold.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-slate-50 p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-100 mb-6">
        <label class="flex justify-between text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 mb-2">
            <span id="sp-stage-label">Two Separate Groups</span>
        </label>
        <input type="range" id="sp-slider" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="0" class="w-full accent-purple-600">
        <div class="flex justify-between text-xs text-slate-400 mt-1">
            <span>Separate groups</span>
            <span>Group trends</span>
            <span>Combined</span>
            <span>Reversed trend</span>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div id="plot7" class="w-full h-96"></div>
    <div id="sp-result" class="mt-4 text-center p-4 bg-purple-50 text-purple-900 rounded-lg text-lg"></div>
    <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-4">
        This scenario is a standard textbook way of demonstrating Simpson's Paradox with made-up
        data. The same reversal shows up in real studies too. For example, in a
        1986 <em>British Medical Journal</em> comparison of kidney stone treatments, Treatment A
        outperformed Treatment B on both small stones (93% vs. 87% success) and large stones (73% vs.
        69% success), yet Treatment B appeared to win overall (83% vs. 78%) once the case mix was
        ignored. Further reading:
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simpson%27s_paradox" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-purple-600 underline hover:text-purple-800">Simpson's Paradox on Wikipedia</a>.
    </p>
</ParadoxLayout>

<script>
    import '../scripts/paradoxes/simpsons-paradox.js';
</script>
